  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>The formula to calculate the speed of a satellite while revolving around the Earth is given by</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><mi>R</mi></mfrac></msqrt><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mfenced><mn>1</mn></mfenced></math></p><p>where,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>v</mi></math>&nbsp;is the speed,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>G</mi></math>&nbsp;is the Gravitational constant,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></math>&nbsp;is the mass of the Earth,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math>&nbsp;is the radius of the orbit.</p><p>As can be seen from the equation, speed is&nbsp;independent of mass of satellite.</p><p>Hence, the speed of the satellites will be the same.</p></blockquote>",
    "image": null
  },
